#{[17592186227241 :answer/value "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ |\n| $3$ | $17$ |\n| $4$ | $22$ |"] [17592186227242 :answer/value "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $11$ |"] [17592186227243 :answer/value "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $9$ |\n| $3$ | $13$ |\n| $4$ | $17$ |"] [17592186228745 :question/worked-solution "To create a frequency table, we first count the number of times each value appears in the data set. This count is known as the frequency of a value.\n\n- The frequency of $6$ is $4$ because the value $6$ appears $4$ times in the data set.\n- The frequency of $7$ is $1$ because the value $7$ appears $1$ time in the data set.\n- The frequency of $8$ is $2$ because the value $8$ appears $2$ times in the data set.\n- The frequency of $9$ is $3$ because the value $9$ appears $3$ times in the data set.\n\nTherefore, the frequency table is as follows:\n\n| Number | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $9$ | $3$ |"] [17592186228746 :question/problem "A librarian recorded the number of books read by each student in a reading club. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Student | Books read |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 1 | $5$ |\n| 2 | $8$ |\n| 3 | $6$ |\n| 4 | $9$ |\n| 5 | $4$ |\n| 6 | $7$ |\n| 7 | $10$ |\n\nWhat is the range of the number of books read among the $7$ students?"] [17592186228747 :question/problem "| Streaming platform | Average monthly users (millions) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Platform X | $215$ |\n| Platform Y | $160$ |\n| Platform Z | $95$ |\n\nBased on the table above, how many more million users does Platform X have compared to Platform Z?"] [17592186228748 :question/problem "| Rent | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $3$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $10$ | $5$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |\n| $15$ | $1$ |\n\nThe table above shows the monthly rental costs (in hundreds of dollars) for $20$ units in an apartment complex. Which of the following gives the correct order of the mean, median, and mode of the rent amounts?\n\n1. $\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}$\n2. $\\text{median} < \\text{mean}$\n3. $\\text{mean} < \\text{median}$\n4. $\\text{median} = \\text{mean} = \\text{mode}$"] [17592186228749 :question/problem "The table below summarizes the frequency of values in a data set.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $99$ | $3$ |\n| $101$ | $2$ |\n| $98$ | $4$ |\n| $100$ | $1$ |\n| $97$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the minimum value of the data set?"] [17592186228750 :question/problem "The table below shows the number of daily users for each of the $35$ different mobile apps reviewed in a tech report.\n\n| Number of daily users | Number of apps |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $100$ to $199$ | $5$ |\n| $200$ to $299$ | $4$ |\n| $300$ to $399$ | $6$ |\n| $400$ to $499$ | $5$ |\n| $500$ to $599$ | $6$ |\n| $600$ to $699$ | $5$ |\n| $700$ to $799$ | $4$ |\n\nWhich of the following could be the median number of daily users per app?\n\n1. $450$\n2. $350$\n3. $600$\n4. $250$"] [17592186228751 :question/problem "| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $9$ | $z$ |\n| $19$ | $3z$ |\n| $29$ | $6z$ |\n| $39$ | $3z$ |\n| $49$ | $z$ |\n\nThe frequency table above displays a set of data, where $z$ is a positive integer. By how much does the mean of the data set exceed the median?"] [17592186228752 :question/problem "Each of the following frequency tables represents a data set. Which one has the greatest mean? Round to two decimal places where necessary.\n\n**I.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |\n\n**II.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $2$ |\n\n**III.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $1$ |\n\n**IV.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $1$ |"] [17592186228752 :question/worked-solution "The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nWe proceed to determine the mean of the data sets from each of the given frequency tables.\n\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$1$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(1) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{61}{8}; \\approx 7.63.\n$$\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$2$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(2))/(8); = \\frac{52}{8}; = 6.5.\n$$\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$3$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(3) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{43}{8}; \\approx 5.38.\n$$\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$3$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(2) + 5(3) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{46}{8}; = 5.75.\n$$\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$1$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(1) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{55}{8}; \\approx 6.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the frequency table whose data set has the greatest mean is as follows:\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |"] [17592186228762 :question/problem "A museum surveyed visitors about four exhibits, asking each to choose the one they enjoyed most. Each response was submitted either at an on-site kiosk or via an email link. The table below shows the distribution of preferences, in percent, by response method. The total number of email responses was four times the number of kiosk responses.\n\n|  | Kiosk | Email |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Sculpture | $18\\%$ | $22\\%$ |\n| Painting | $37\\%$ | $28\\%$ |\n| Science | $22\\%$ | $30\\%$ |\n| History | $23\\%$ | $20\\%$ |\n| **Total** | **$100\\%$** | **$100\\%$** |\n\nIf a visitor who preferred the sculpture exhibit is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor responded via email?"] [17592186228764 :question/problem "An electronics shop records whether a buyer’s main purchase was a laptop or a tablet, and how the order was placed. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $14$ | $x$ |\n\nIf a person who bought a tablet is selected at random, the probability that the person ordered by online is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186228764 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $x$, we use the given conditional probability: the probability that a person ordered by online, given they bought a tablet, is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThe conditional probability is calculated as the ratio of the number of people who ordered by phone and bought a tablet to the total number of people who bought a tablet. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of tablet buyers who ordered by online}}{\\text{Total number who bought a tablet}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are\n\n$$\n21 + 14 + x = {\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\npeople who bought a tablet. Of these, ${\\color{blue}14}$ buyers ordered online.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $\\color{blue}14$ | $x$ |\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}14}{\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\nSince this must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{14}{35 + x} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{35 + x}{14} &= \\frac{6}{1} \\\\\n35 + x &= 84 \\\\\nx &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228765 :question/problem "A group of $30$ students, during each of $3$ different practice exams, answered $5$ questions. Each correct response was worth $1$ point. The table shows how many students earned each possible score on each exam.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nNo student received the same score on two different exams. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student earned a perfect score on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$, given that the student earned a perfect score on one of the three exams?"] [17592186228765 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of students who received a score of $5$ on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$ to the total number of students who received a score of $5$ on one of the three exams. This is given by\n$1$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of ${\\color{red}12}$ students received a score of $5$. Of this amount,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{purple}4} = 10\n$$\n\nstudents received that score in either Exam $1$ or Exam $3$.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $\\color{purple}4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | ${\\color{red}12}$ | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Exam1or3∣Perfect Score) &= \\frac{6 + 4}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228766 :question/problem "The table below shows the number of teachers in the English and History departments, categorized by whether they teach honors classes.\n\n|  | English | History |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Teach honors | $8$ | $5$ |\n| Do not teach honors | $4$ | $7$ |\n\nOne teacher from the English or History department is selected at random. If the teacher is from the English department, what is the probability that they do not teach honors classes?"] [17592186228766 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of English teachers who do not teach honors to the total number of English teachers. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Not honors} \\mid \\text{English})=\\frac{\\text{Number of English not teaching honors}}{\\text{Total number of English}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of\n\n$$\n8+4={\\color{red}12}\n$$\n\nEnglish teachers in the department. Of this amount, ${\\color{blue}4}$ do not teach honors.\n\n|  | English | History |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Teach honors | $8$ | $5$ |\n| Do not teach honors | $\\color{blue}4$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( \\text{Not honors} ∣ \\text{English} ) & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {\\color{blue}4}}{ {\\color{red}12}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\cdot  4}{3 \\cdot  4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\cdot  \\cancel{4}}{3 \\cdot  \\cancel{4}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1}{3} .\\end{array}}\n$$"] [17592186228767 :question/problem "A veterinary clinic tracked incoming calls on a single day, recording whether callers inquired about appointments or pet food. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Pet food request | No pet food request | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Appointment request | $55$ | $345$ | $400$ |\n| No Appointment request | $45$ | $55$ | $100$ |\n| **Total** | **$100$** | **$400$** | **$500$** |\n\nIf a caller who asked about pet food is selected at random, what is the probability that the caller also asked about an appointment?"] [17592186228767 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of callers who asked about pet food and an appointment to the total number of callers who asked about pet food. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Appointment} \\mid \\text{Pet food}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of requests for appointment and pet food}}{\\text{Total number of requests for pet food}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are a total of ${\\color{red}100}$ callers who asked about pet food. Of this amount, ${\\color{blue}55}$ also asked about an appointment.\n\n|  | Pet food request | No pet food request | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Appointment request | $\\color{blue}55$ | $345$ | $400$ |\n| No Appointment request | $45$ | $55$ | $100$ |\n| **Total** | ${\\color{red}100}$ | **$400$** | **$500$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Appointment∣Pet food) &= \\frac{55}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228771 :question/problem "In a midterm exam, each student receives a score between $50$ and $100$. The table below shows the score distribution for the $13$ students in Class $M$ and the $11$ students in Class $N$.\n\n| Score | Class $M$ | Class $N$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $50$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $55$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $60$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $65$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $70$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n| $75$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $80$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $90$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $100$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| Total | $13$ | $11$ |\n\nThe median score for Class $N$ is how many points greater than the median score for Class $M$?"] [17592186228772 :question/problem "| Value | Data set A frequency | Data set B frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $12$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $16$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n| $20$ | $7$ | $6$ |\n| $24$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| $28$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nData set $A$ and data set $B$ each contain $27$ values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets? Round to two decimal places where necessary.\n\n1. The mean of the data set $A$ is less than the mean of the data set $B$.\n2. The mean of the data set $A$ is equal to the mean of the data set $B$.\n3. The mean of the data set $A$ is greater than the mean of the data set $B$.\n4. There is not enough information to compare the means of the two datasets."] [17592186228774 :question/problem "| Museum | $2012$ | $2013$ | $2014$ | $2015$ | $2016$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| North Museum | $540$ | $460$ | $520$ | $500$ | $480$ |\n| South Museum | $300$ | $480$ | $500$ | $510$ | $520$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of visitors at two museums in each of the five years. No visitor was counted at both museums.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the number of visitors over the $5$ years shown?\n\n1. The mean number from North Museum is greater than the mean number from South Museum.\n2. The median number from North Museum is greater than the median number from South Museum."] [17592186228801 :question/problem "Veterinarians sometimes estimate a dog's age in years by multiplying its weight in pounds by a constant growth factor that varies depending on the breed. The table below shows the growth factors for several dog breeds.\n\n| Dog Breed | Growth<br>factor |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Labrador Retriever | $5$ |\n| German Shepherd | $5.5$ |\n| Golden Retriever | $5$ |\n| Bulldog | $5$ |\n| Beagle | $4$ |\n| Boxer | $5.5$ |\n| Poodle | $4.5$ |\n| Rottweiler | $6$ |\n\nSuppose a Golden Retriever and a Poodle each currently weigh $14$ pounds. Which of the following is closest to the difference, in pounds, between their weights $11$ years from now?"] [17592186228828 :question/problem "Two towns grow as summarized in the table. The population of City A grows by a percentage each year.\n\n|  | Initial population | Yearly increase |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| City A | $50{,}000$ | $3\\%$ per year (applied to current population) |\n| City B | $55{,}000$ | $1{,}200$ people per year |\n\nWhich of the following is true about the populations?\n\n1. City A always gains more people per year than City B.\n2. City A gains more people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains less people per year.\n3. City A always gains less people per year than City B.\n4. City A gains less people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains more people per year."] [17592186228829 :question/problem "The table shows selected values from the function $k$.\n\n| $x$ | $k(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |\n| $2$ | $-9$ |\n\nWhich of the following is the best description of function $k$?\n\n1. Decreasing exponential\n2. Increasing exponential\n3. Increasing linear\n4. Decreasing linear"] [17592186228841 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n7+5+3+2+2=19\n$$\n\nSince this is an odd number, the median is the value in position\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{19+1}{2}=\\dfrac{20}{2}=10\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the first row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $10$th value falls in the second interval, which is\n$18 - 21$\nas shown below.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the median is $18$."] [17592186228842 :question/problem "| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ |\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the largest possible value of the mean?"] [17592186228842 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the largest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{max}}$ | $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ | $25$ | $4\\cdot 25 = 100$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ | $28$ | $7\\cdot 28 = 196$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ | $31$ | $2\\cdot 31 = 62$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ | $34$ | $5\\cdot 34 = 170$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ | $37$ | $3\\cdot 37 = 111$ |\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlargest possible mean &= \\frac{100 + 196 + 62 + 170 + 111}{4 + 7 + 2 + 5 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{639}{21} \\\\\n&\\approx 30.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186228843 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the smallest possible range, we take the smallest possible maximum value and subtract the largest possible minimum value.\n\nLet's start by turning our histogram into a frequency table.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $30-\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{35}$ | $6$ |\n| $36-37$ | $8$ |\n| $38-40$ | $10$ |\n| $41-43$ | $7$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{44}-51$ | $4$ |\n\nLooking at the table,\n\n- the smallest possible maximum value is $44$, and\n- the largest possible minimum value is $35$.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible range is\n\n$$\n44 - 35 = 9\n$$"] [17592186228845 :question/problem "| **Ivy** | $3.6$ | $3.8$ | $4.0$ | $3.9$ | $4.2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Jack** | $4.4$ | $4.2$ | $4.0$ | $3.8$ | $x$ |\n\nIvy and Jack each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Jack’s attempts was $0.2$ greater than the mean score for Ivy’s attempts. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186228855 :question/problem "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n|  | Under $40$ | $40$ and Over |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Support | $370$ | $380$ |\n| Do Not Support | ? | $300$ |\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?"] [17592186228864 :question/problem "A table shows the average annual rainfall in a region for six different years.\n\n| Year | Average rainfall (cm) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1985$ | $100$ |\n| $1995$ | $120$ |\n| $2005$ | $150$ |\n| $2010$ | $180$ |\n| $2015$ | $200$ |\n| $2025$ | $220$ |\n\nWhat was the percentage increase in rainfall from $1995$ to $2010$?"] [17592186228865 :question/problem "A total of $34,\\!852$ students study at a certain university. The university conducted a survey among a randomly chosen sample of $1,\\!200$ students about their preference for morning or evening classes. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Morning | Evening | No Preference | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Freshmen and Sophomores | $280$ | $190$ | $30$ | $500$ |\n| Juniors and Seniors | $210$ | $150$ | $40$ | $400$ |\n| Graduates | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ | $300$ |\n| Total | $610$ | $440$ | $150$ | $1,\\!200$ |\n\nWhat is the difference between the percentage of undergraduates (freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors combined) who preferred morning classes and the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes? Rounded to the nearest whole number."] [17592186228866 :question/problem "A hospital tracked the number of patient visits in two consecutive years, as shown below.\n\n| Year | Patient visits |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2020$ | $30,\\!000$ |\n| $2021$ | $31,\\!500$ |\n\nThe hospital administrator estimated that the percentage increase from $2021$ to $2022$ would be four times the percentage increase from $2020$ to $2021$. How many visits did the administrator expect in $2022$?"] [17592186229285 :question/problem "| Response | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Every day | $5$ |\n| Several times a week | $12$ |\n| Once a week | $22$ |\n| A few times a month | $58$ |\n| Rarely | $47$ |\n| Never | $36$ |\n| Total | $180$ |\n\nThe table above shows results from a survey of $180$ students who were asked how often they exercise outdoors. How many students answered either \"rarely\" or \"never\"?"] [17592186229286 :question/problem "| Type of facility | Average number of staff |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Hospital | $420$ |\n| Clinic | $95$ |\n| Urgent care center | $145$ |\n\nBased on the table above, how many more staff members does a hospital have on average compared to a clinic?"] [17592186229302 :question/problem "| Data value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $5$ |\n| $6$ | $7$ |\n| $7$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $10$ |\n| $9$ | $8$ |\n| $10$ | $5$ |\n| $11$ | $0$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n\nThe table above summarizes the $47$ data values in a data set. What is the maximum data value in the data set?\n\nThe maximum data value is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186229305 :question/problem "The table below summarizes the frequency of values in a data set.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $15$ | $3$ |\n| $17$ | $6$ |\n| $18$ | $5$ |\n| $20$ | $2$ |\n\nWhat is the minimum value of the data set?\n\nThe minimum data value is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186229334 :question/problem "A quality control inspector recorded the number of defective batteries produced during $10$ different shifts. The data are displayed in the table below.\n\n| Shift | Number of defective batteries |\n| --- | ---: |\n| A | $12$ |\n| B | $15$ |\n| C | $13$ |\n| D | $11$ |\n| E | $14$ |\n| F | $15$ |\n| G | $16$ |\n| H | $13$ |\n| I | $12$ |\n| J | $14$ |\n\nWhat is the range of the number of defective batteries produced across all $10$ shifts?"] [17592186229335 :question/problem "A librarian recorded the number of books read by each student in a reading club. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Student | Books read |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 1 | $5$ |\n| 2 | $8$ |\n| 3 | $6$ |\n| 4 | $9$ |\n| 5 | $4$ |\n| 6 | $7$ |\n| 7 | $10$ |\n\nWhat is the range of the number of books read among the $7$ students?"] [17592186229338 :question/problem "| Salary | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $70$ | $1$ |\n| $75$ | $2$ |\n| $80$ | $4$ |\n| $85$ | $5$ |\n| $90$ | $4$ |\n| $95$ | $3$ |\n| $140$ | $1$ |\n\nThe table above shows the distribution of salaries (in thousands of dollars) of $20$ employees at a small design firm. Which of the following gives the correct order of the mean, median, and mode of the salaries?"] [17592186229339 :question/problem "| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $12$ | $a$ |\n| $24$ | $3a$ |\n| $36$ | $4a$ |\n| $48$ | $3a$ |\n| $60$ | $a$ |\n\nThe frequency table above displays a set of data, where $a$ is a positive integer. By how much does the mean of the data set exceed the median?"] [17592186229340 :question/problem "| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $9$ | $z$ |\n| $19$ | $3z$ |\n| $29$ | $6z$ |\n| $39$ | $3z$ |\n| $49$ | $z$ |\n\nThe frequency table above displays a set of data, where 